#{[17592186083302 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186083772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275314 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186401055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186083102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083302 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186084250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1045 "The Arc Length of a Planar Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186083450 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f0036e42-d6e1-5a92-99f0-f7897f818532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1061 1974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e30a6e68-0f69-5f3c-9066-8d1faed363f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084092 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1061 1975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "503684ad-7ed7-5631-b233-b8294b8c6b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084093 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1061 1979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a3b431a-28d4-5ffa-84fe-0570a5b35b3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084096 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1061 26917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83509829-52cc-54fc-aff4-e35b85b466d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084236 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084250 #edb/ref 1061 28187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084250 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b91df088-62b4-5fea-901c-a934dc9c4398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084250 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we're trying to find the **arc length** of a curve $y=f(x)$ between two points where $x=a$ and $x=b.$ The arc length of a typical function between two points is shown below:\n\n![](images/e7/e7fb3f6e11e0e2b51fc4ecae9827a4e378a7981450775f2b9330057c319688bf.png)\n\nIn this image, the solid line represents the length that we're trying to find, and the dotted line represents the continuation of the curve $y=f(x)$ beyond the points of interest.\n\nThe arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between the points where $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1065 1006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c6cef2d6-0611-5e58-b127-47c3b537067e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084388 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1064 "We've been using the following formula for the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between the points where $x=a$ and $x=b{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+\\left[f'(x)\\right]^2}\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nFirst, we divide the curve into small subintervals. Consider a part of the blue arc that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of that part be $\\Delta{s}$.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b4d9b2e66ae976ac136170f13525240b02f3f10b3887d6b19738088be528ff.png)\n\nWe can now denote the horizontal displacement as $\\Delta{x}$ and the vertical shift as $\\Delta{y}.$ \n\n![](images/7f/7fe992a709634e9be41f8a2c6d12f7bc72c6f9e8105c19f5f5a47e98c3673b06.png)\n\nTo approximate the length $\\Delta{s}$ we apply the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δs\\approx \\sqrt{{(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2}}=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{Δy}{Δx})}^{2}}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo approximate the total length $L,$ we need to sum up all of the little $\\Delta s$ terms, so\n\n$$\nL \n\\approx \\sum \\Delta s = \\sum \\sqrt{\\left( \\Delta{x} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\Delta{y} \\right)^2}\n= \\sum \\left( \\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\Delta{y}}{\\Delta{x}} \\right)^2} \\cdot \\Delta{x} \\right).\n$$\n\nWe then take the limit as $\\Delta x, \\Delta y \\rightarrow 0.$ As we do this, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}s & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{'}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nthe sum becomes a definite integral, and our approximation approaches the exact value of $L{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\int \\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{'}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1065 4576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8eece214-0f4a-5143-bf64-d939687b9276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084473 #edb/ref 1067 "The Intuition Behind the Main Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1039 "Computing Variance Using the Definition Given a Probability Mass Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186105657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105823 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4f8fd8a4-1ca2-521d-8542-d11311bcc586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105824 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105824 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106504 #edb/ref 175