1038 #edb/ref 17592186372007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179773 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179773 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Area Scale Factor of an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179773 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179773 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1577f765-f98c-5020-8e45-8da2c5f092e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1045 "The Image of an Affine Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179859 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c9655728-f410-56c7-8943-6d053246e392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-124004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179988 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = v - u, y = u + v - 1$.\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) =-2$\n3. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44bbbc90-f4f2-5074-bbff-3d0cc0edc535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180553 #edb/ref 1061 44648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e42cdff-0162-5f00-9062-0bbf4e7ef4c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180554 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180554 #edb/ref 1061 44649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "130575e3-c9fd-5688-a557-9be7454f3b61" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180555 #edb/ref 1061 44652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "804e3ae7-0f27-5ff7-9101-bf7c7b3dfa01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180755 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180556 #edb/ref 1061 44653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3f2a959e-2593-532e-916d-db411d030416" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180557 #edb/ref 1061 44654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfcdd1b6-82a4-5529-8d93-b859895ed88c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180558 #edb/ref 1061 44657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "db19ad89-86be-5bd4-8aba-82b5b0c23c42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180564 #edb/ref 1061 44685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180564 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6e8df5a8-690a-5bd7-acaa-c40815a5fca6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180564 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1061 44703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c8f36a3-dfdb-59b6-82cb-0413df0361cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180754 #edb/ref 1064 "To find the image of a point under an affine transformation, all we need to do is substitute the position vector of the point into the formula for the affine transformation.\n\nFor example, consider the affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\Bbb R^2 \\to \\Bbb R^2,$ defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSuppose that the point $P({\\color{black}-1},{\\color{black}1})$ with position vector $\\mathbf p$ lies in the $uv$-plane, as shown below. \n\n![](images/06/06c9283bde54e329d31d5892702e4a75c0ca78bd21ee3bb23f9a2b748239bf14.png)\n\nWe can find the image of $P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by substituting $\\mathbf p$ into the affine transformation formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(P) & =Ap+b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $P$ is the point $P'({\\color{black}-1},{\\color{black}3})$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe can visualize $P$ and its image under $\\mathbf T$ using a diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/89/893933990225c54e0a3327de70f29b3d73af2b829cf53053d155bb52d5202e63.png)\n\nWe can use a similar method to map multiple points from the $uv$-plane to the $xy$-plane using just one calculation. Let's see how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180754 #edb/ref 1065 7694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180754 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b71d5e38-7267-5428-89f5-941492316127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180754 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\Delta$ with sides $S_1, S_2,$ and $S_3,$  shown below. Let's imagine that we traverse the boundary once in the counterclockwise direction starting from the origin.\n\n![](images/23/231dd5f96cd36d21940fcee894382edc9eabc91b0bf72bb4a8724c8444f76a8c.png)\n\nLet the affine transformation $\\mathbf T$ be defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle, presented in the order shown in the diagram:\n![](images/34/34be6d7cd24938fb72976910cf3ad6a790eb93574915829f621da99f4aec3838.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $\\mathbf b$ in each column:\n![](images/be/bed30fcc6a1003bee7f2f7888af9c301314b2aba351497db35cf2ee37710c0a1.svg)\n\nThe image of $\\mathbf u$ under $T$ is given by $A\\mathbf u + \\mathbf b.$ So, by applying $\\mathbf T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vertices of the image are given by the columns of the above matrix. Drawing these vertices in the order shown, i.e.\n\n$$\n(1,-1) \\to (1,0) \\to (2,-1),\n$$\n\nwe get the following picture:\n\n![](images/d5/d514834e6e9f108f478002416e863cf9c98d00add230c2e9321664ea14e5db0e.png)\n\n          \n\nNotice that the polygon $D$ is traversed in the *clockwise* direction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1065 7695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c717442d-1c85-597b-bfc9-3bacde35a1f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180755 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the Image of a Polygon" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
